it all looks good! but I do not undesrtand why you use:
forDelay + numberOfLaterals*rp->multDelay;
instead of:
forDelay * numberOfLaterals * rp->multDelay;
or :
forDelay * (numberOfLaterals+1) * rp->multDelay;

> it all looks good! but I do not undesrtand why you use: forDelay + numberOfLaterals*rp->multDelay; instead of: forDelay * numberOfLaterals * rp->multDelay; or : forDelay * (numberOfLaterals+1) * rp->multDelay;

Dear Mona, I probably did not get your original idea, my thinking was: 
* each organ needs its own time to develop given by (forDelay); 
* the start of this is triggered in dependence on the organs of that emerge before, where we don't add up their specific emergence delay, but for simplicity assume a constant (multDelay), that is the mean delay we add per preceding organ (so multDelay = 0, all start at the same time, multDelay = forDelay, they start one after another)

Actually, I would prefer  forDelay + numberOfLaterals*rp->multDelay* **forDelay**; (then multDelay = 0 is same time, multDelay = 1 is sequential)

But maybe forDelay * numberOfLaterals * rp->multDelay; is better, what is the idea behind it? Best, Daniel
